## Read order (mandatory)

When any agent consults the wiki:

1. Read `_hot.md` first (~400 tokens; current state, open OWNER DECISIONS, recent demotions, watchlist patterns, pending queue depth).
2. Read `index.md` second (catalog of every curated page).
3. Read specific files only after the first two have framed the search.

This order is enforced at the schema layer because `_hot.md` is the only file refreshed every Mode B run; reading `index.md` first risks acting on stale signals.

## Hard rules

1. **Never edit `sources/`.** It is the source of truth. If `wiki/` drifts, the recovery path is to rebuild from `sources/`. The only exception is `sources/_pending.md`, whose `[PENDING] → [COMPILED]` markers are flipped by the curator (and only those markers).
2. **Never invent facts.** Every claim in `wiki/` must trace to a file under `sources/` or to the codebase. If you cannot point to a source, do not write it.
3. **Never silently change `confidence:` frontmatter.** Promotions (`medium → high`) are owner-only. Demotions are curator-allowed but always appear in the diff proposal — never silent.
4. **Never write outside `wiki/`** as the curator. Sources are append-only by other tools. Production code is out of scope.
5. **Format consistency outranks novelty.** Imitate the seed exemplars (ADRs, primers, patterns). A "better" idea about format goes in the diff as an OWNER DECISION — never a silent change.

## Curator commands

- `/specflow:wiki-init` — bootstrap or repair this layout (idempotent).
- `/specflow:wiki` — Mode B compilation pass (end-of-sprint).
- Mode A — per-cycle distillation, invoked from the TDD orchestrator (e.g. `triad.md` Phase 8.5).

All three are read-and-propose first, write-only-on-approval. Same shape as the project's REFACTOR gate.

## Frontmatter convention

Every curated file under `wiki/` carries:

```yaml
---
title: <human title>
tags: [<area>, <subdomain>...]
last_updated: <YYYY-MM-DD>
source_issues: [<gh-issue-numbers>]
confidence: <high | medium | low>
---
```

No omissions, no extra fields. The control files (`_hot.md`, `_log.md`, `index.md`, `_pending.md`) follow their own minimal frontmatter — see their respective seed contents.

## Confidence dial

- `high` — owner-confirmed, stable, safe to cite externally.
- `medium` — derived from documented sources but not yet battle-tested.
- `low` — inferred from a single cycle or external article; treat as hypothesis until validated.

Promotion paths require owner approval. Demotion happens automatically when a more recent cycle contradicts a file's claims.

## Categories that feed product documentation

The wiki is structured so that each curated category maps to a downstream documentation surface. This is the project-specific shape of Karpathy's second brain — the wiki is not just engineering memory, it is the upstream feed for help docs, ops docs, compliance docs, and architecture diagrams.

| Category | What it captures | Feeds (downstream) |
|---|---|---|
| `decisions/` | ADRs (300–500 words, five sections) | Engineering rationale; architecture-decision register |
| `domains/` | Internal mental models (six sections) | Engineering onboarding |
| `patterns/` | Reusable mechanics ("Name 3" gate) | Internal best-practice catalog |
| `interfaces/` | What we **expose** — HTTP, TS, GraphQL, CLI, events | Partner docs, SDK reference, public API docs |
| `flows/` | End-to-end user journeys (happy path + branches) | Help center, onboarding, QA scenarios, support |
| `runbooks/` | Operational procedures with verification + rollback | On-call playbook, incident response, admin guides |
| `dependencies/` | External services we **consume**, with criticality + fallback | Architecture diagrams, deps page, blast-radius |
| `policies/` | Non-functional cross-cutting (retention, GDPR, SLOs) | Compliance, trust page, security overview |
| `risks/` | Threat-model entries with severity / likelihood / status | Security overview, audit prep, blast-radius analysis |
| `glossary.md` / `lessons.md` | Terms & cross-cycle anti-patterns | Engineering reference |

The curator surfaces candidates for each category during Mode A (per-cycle) and Mode B (end-of-sprint). Format consistency outranks novelty: every page imitates its template.

## Interfaces — bridge to product documentation

The `interfaces/` category surfaces every public API or contract that consumers (other services, mobile/web clients, partners, external developers) depend on. Each page distils:

- The **contract shape** — endpoint / type / event / CLI signature.
- The **stability** — stable / experimental / deprecated, plus versioning policy.
- The **audience** — internal-only, external-facing, or both.
- The **canonical source** — link to the file that defines it (OpenAPI YAML, TS types, proto, etc.). The page does not replace that file; it summarises it for documentation generation.

This category exists because product documentation is downstream of engineering reality. By keeping interface pages in the wiki, the team always has a single source for "what we expose", confidence-graded and dated, ready to feed product/partner docs without re-discovery.

The curator surfaces interface candidates when:

- A cycle modifies a public/exported function, type, HTTP endpoint, GraphQL field, CLI command, or event payload (Mode A).
- A Mode B scan finds an exported public surface that has no `interfaces/` page yet.
- An interface was renamed, deprecated, or had its stability changed.

Same `confidence` dial as every other curated page; demotion happens automatically when the canonical source diverges from the page.

## "Name 3" rule (patterns only)

A pattern earns a page in `patterns/` only when **3 known consumers** exist in the codebase, each named by file path. Two consumers go on a watchlist; one is just a coincidence. The rule is canonical (`lessons.md` → "Name 3, build once") and the curator enforces it on every Mode B run.

## Schema version

This file's frontmatter `schema_version` pins the layout. If specflow ships a newer version, the next `/specflow:wiki-init` run proposes a migration diff. Do not edit `schema_version` by hand.
